2><p>Below is a list of papers related to chain-of-thought (CoT) prompting.=
 Note that the list is not exhaustive, and I also only focus on 2025 papers=
 from my bookmark list.</p><p><strong><span>31 Jan 2025, s1: Simple Test-Ti=
me Scaling, </span><a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.19393">https://arx=
iv.org/abs/2501.19393</a></strong></p><p>Here, researchers implement a budg=
et-forcing mechanism that appends =E2=80=9CWait=E2=80=9D tokens to prompt t=
he model to emit longer reasoning chains (and truncates it when a given bud=
get is reached).</p><p><strong><span>30 May 2025, AlphaOne: Reasoning Model=
s Thinking Slow and Fast at Test Time, </span><a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/=
abs/2505.24863">https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.24863</a></strong></p><p>This wo=
rk is essentially a modification of the s1 method discussed above. They def=
ine an =E2=80=9C=CE=B1-moment=E2=80=9D (=E2=80=9Daha=E2=80=9D-moment) that =
expands the thinking budget, then stochastically insert =E2=80=9Cwait=E2=80=
=9D tokens before that point to trigger slow reasoning (where slow reasonin=
g means generating more tokens to expand the answer). They then finally for=
ce a shift into fast reasoning (i.e., generating short, very direct respons=
es) by converting later =E2=80=9Cwait=E2=80=9D tokens into =E2=80=9C&lt;/th=
ink&gt;=E2=80=9D. This so-called =E2=80=9Cslow-then-fast=E2=80=9D schedule =
improves accuracy across math, coding, and science tasks.</p><p><strong><sp=
an>25 Feb 2025, Chain of Draft: Thinking Faster by Writing Less, </span><a =
href=3D"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.18600">https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.18600<=
/a></strong></p><p>This method works similarly to CoT prompting, but it aim=
s to reduce the length of the intermediate reasoning chain by focusing more=
 on minimalist intermediate drafting (like we humans do, for instance, when=
 we scribble down intermediate steps when working on a math problem).</p><p=
><strong><span>10 Feb 2025, ReasonFlux: Hierarchical LLM Reasoning via Scal=
ing Thought Templates, </span><a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.06772">=
https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.06772</a></strong></p><p>Where CoT shifts the mo=
del into a =E2=80=9Creasoning mode,=E2=80=9D this ReasonFlux method shifts =
the model into a =E2=80=9Ctemplate-guided reasoning mode.=E2=80=9D I.e., it=
 uses different types of prompt templates based on the problem to elicit re=
asoning. (Caveat: It=E2=80=99s not a pure inference-scaling method as it do=
es require training the model to use these structured templates.)</p><p><st=
rong><span>8 Jan 2025, Towards System 2 Reasoning in LLMs: Learning How to =
Think With Meta Chain-of-Thought, </span><a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/abs/2=
501.04682">https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.04682</a></strong></p><p>Meta-CoT is =
an extension of CoT =E2=80=9Cby explicitly modeling the underlying reasonin=
g required to arrive at a particular CoT=E2=80=9D.</p><p>This is again a me=
thod that requires some additional training before it can be applied as an =
 inference scaling method.</p><p>How it works is that after training, the m=
odel does not just emit a linear CoT, but it emulates the latent search pro=
cess that underlies it. This means exploring branches, backtracking, and ve=
rifying intermediate states, etc., before committing to a final solution.</=
p><p>I am grouping it here as an extension of CoT, but I could also have ad=
ded it to either the self-refine or tree-of-thought categories below. In th=
is case, the grouping is not clear-cut. We can roughly think of the differe=
nt methods as</p><ul><li><p>CoT =3D one path</p></li><li><p>Tree-of-thought=
 =3D many paths explored externally (outside the model=E2=80=99s forward pa=
th)</p></li><li><p>Meta-CoT =3D many paths explored internally.</p></li></u=
l><h1 class=3D"header-anchor-post">3. Self-Consistency<div class=3D"pencraf=
