earch: Efficient Test-Time Scaling in Large Language Models, </span><a href=
=3D"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.18149">https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.18149</a><=
/strong></p><p>In this paper, the authors leverage the observation that rea=
soning tasks with shorter traces are much more likely to be correct than th=
ose with longer ones, as well as the fact that self-consistency can be effi=
ciently parallelized across multiple devices, since each query runs indepen=
dently.</p><p><span>So, here, the idea is that they launch </span><em>n</em=
><span> generation processes, and return as soon as one completes. That=E2=
=80=99s it.</span></p><p><strong><span>21 May, Learning to Reason via Mixtu=
re-of-Thought for Logical Reasoning, </span><a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/ab=
s/2505.15817">https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.15817</a></strong></p><p>At first =
glance, MoT looks superficially similar to self-consistency because the fin=
al answer comes from multiple reasoning traces and may be determined by maj=
ority vote (or a scoring method). However, unlike regular self-consistency,=
 they aim to sample multiple reasoning modalities. E.g., &lt;code&gt; ... &=
lt;/code&gt;, &lt;truth_table&gt; =E2=80=A6 &lt;/truth_table&gt;, etc, foll=
owed by majority voting among the results. Note that this is applied at inf=
erence time, but it also requires training the model to output these differ=
ent modalities.</p><p><strong>1 Dec,</strong><span> </span><strong>The Art =
of Scaling Test-Time Compute for Large Language Models,</strong><span> </sp=
an><strong><a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.02008">https://arxiv.org/a=
bs/2512.02008</a></strong></p><p>This paper takes a systematic look at infe=
rence-time scaling strategies and evaluates them across eight modern LLMs (=
7B to 235B), including reasoning models (DeepSeek-R1, DAPO-32B, QwQ-32B, Qw=
en3-32B, GPT-OSS-120B) and non-reasoning models (DeepSeek-Chat, Qwen3-235B)=
. </p><p>The conclusion is that there is no universally best inference-scal=
ing strategy. The optimal choice depends on the model=E2=80=99s reasoning a=
bilities, compute budget, and (surprisingly) not the difficulty of the prob=
lem. </p><p>Self-consistency (here called =E2=80=9Cmajority voting=E2=80=9D=
) performs surprisingly well, though.</p><h1 class=3D"header-anchor-post">4=
