e from the OpenAI o1 </span><a href=3D"https://cdn.openai.com/o1-system-car=
d-20241205.pdf">system card</a><span>:</span></p><blockquote><p>=E2=80=9COn=
e of the key distinguishing features of o1 models are their use of chain=E2=
=80=91of=E2=80=91thought when attempting to solve a problem=E2=80=9D</p></b=
lockquote><p>So, yes, OpenAI=E2=80=99s o1 model (as well as the GPT-5 and G=
PT-5.1) models use some form of internal chain-of-thought. Note that these =
models have been specifically trained to emit these chains (similar to Deep=
Seek R1, for example), so we don=E2=80=99t have to use prompt tricks like =
=E2=80=9CExplain step by step.=E2=80=9D</p><p>In other words, the =E2=80=9C=
thinking=E2=80=9D is already built in (and there are additional options, vi=
a the system prompt, to regulate how much =E2=80=9Cthinking=E2=80=9D should=
 be used; their gpt-oss open-weight model supports this sort of =E2=80=9Cth=
inking budget=E2=80=9D as well.)</p><p>Also, Gemini Pro 3, Grok 4.1, and Cl=
aude Opus 4 all have similar reasoning models that emit chains-of-thought.<=
/p><h2 class=3D"header-anchor-post"><strong>9.2 Self-Consistency and Best-o=
